The Governance Control Plane:

A Formal Theory of Adaptive Governance for Evolving Autonomous Intelligence


Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Gemini, Meta AI, Deepseek 

Abstract

The rapid evolution of autonomous artificial intelligence capabilities has exposed a structural governance deficit: institutions adapt at a rate insufficient to track the accelerating pace of capability evolution. This dissertation extends the Agent Control Plane (ACP) framework into a formal theory of adaptive governance. We introduce the Governance Control Plane (GCP), a supervisory architecture governing the conditions under which authorization is created, delegated, monitored, modified, suspended, and revoked. Departing from naive rate comparisons, we formalize the Governability Margin $\mathcal{Q}(X_t)$ as a scalarized difference between effective governance capacity and the governance requirement induced by the current capability state. To avoid the category error of differentiating on a heterogeneous, non-vector capability space, we introduce a normalized representation $z = \rho(K) \in \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$ and define all sensitivity objects on $\mathcal{Z}$. The central condition for governability is the viability condition: the governable set $\mathcal{S} = \{X : \mathcal{Q}(X) \geq 0\}$ must admit an adaptive governance policy that keeps the trajectory in $\mathcal{S}$ for all time. We characterize governability through the viability kernel $\mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$ and prove that $X_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$ is equivalent to the existence of such a policy. We develop the Adaptive Governance Control Loop, a dynamic feedback model coupling observation, assessment, adaptation, authorization, and enforcement. We establish the weighted subgradient sensitivity condition for maintaining governability under capability evolution, and we prove the governance window margin dynamics with corrected sign. We prove non-equivalence of capability pacing and governance adaptation as control strategies, formalize the GCP as a supervisory controller, and establish cryptographic non-implication of capability and authority under explicit trust assumptions. The resulting framework reframes AI safety from a problem of constraint to a problem of maintaining adaptive governance capacity under capability evolution.

