2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Standing Assumptions and Notation

We state the standing assumptions governing the development.

Assumption 2.1 (Finiteness of Primitives). In any deployment domain, the primitive sets $\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{M}$ are finite.

Assumption 2.2 (Authority Lattice Finiteness). The authority facet set $\mathcal{F}$ is finite, and the authority lattice $(2^{\mathcal{F}}, \subseteq)$ has height $|\mathcal{F}|$.

Assumption 2.3 (Regularity of Governing Functions). The functions $G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}, H, D, I$, defined on the normalized representation $\mathcal{Z}$ and the environment space, are Lipschitz continuous on compact subsets of their domains.

Assumption 2.4 (Normalized Capability Representation). There exists a map $\rho : \mathcal{K} \to \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$ that is injective on the deployment-relevant subset of $\mathcal{K}$, continuous, and bi-Lipschitz with respect to the capability metric $d_{\mathcal{K}}$ and the Euclidean metric on $\mathbb{R}^p$.

Assumption 2.5 (Marchaud Property of Governance Dynamics). The set-valued map $X \mapsto F_G(X, U(X), \cdot)$ is upper semicontinuous with compact convex values and satisfies a linear growth condition.

Notation. We write $\mathbf{1}_A$ for the indicator of a set $A$, $\partial f(x)$ for the Clarke subdifferential of $f$ at $x$, and $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle$ for the Euclidean inner product.

2.2 Capability Space and Normalized Representation

Definition 2.1 (Capability State). The capability state of agent $i$ at time $t$ is

K_i(t) = (T_i, R_i, B_i, I_i, U_i, H_i, N_i, M_i)

where $T_i \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ (available tools), $R_i \subseteq \mathcal{R}$ (accessible resources), $B_i \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ (computational budget), $I_i \subseteq \mathcal{I}$ (accessible information), $U_i \subseteq \mathcal{U}$ (action repertoire), $H_i \in \mathbb{N}$ (planning horizon), $N_i \in \mathbb{N}$ (replication capability), and $M_i \subseteq \mathcal{M}$ (communication channels). The capability space is

\mathcal{K} = 2^{\mathcal{T}} \times 2^{\mathcal{R}} \times \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \times 2^{\mathcal{I}} \times 2^{\mathcal{U}} \times \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \times 2^{\mathcal{M}}.

Definition 2.2 (Capability Metric). A capability metric is

d_{\mathcal{K}}(K, K') = \sum_{j=1}^{8} w_j \, d_j(K_j, K'_j)

with weights $w_j > 0$, $\sum_j w_j = 1$, and component distances:

d_J(A, B) = \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } A = B = \emptyset \\ 1 - \dfrac{|A \cap B|}{|A \cup B|} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

d_{\mathbb{R}}(x, y) = \frac{|x - y|}{1 + |x - y|}, \qquad d_{\mathbb{N}}(m, n) = \frac{|m - n|}{1 + |m - n|}.

Proposition 2.1. The function $d_{\mathcal{K}}$ is a metric on $\mathcal{K}$.

Proof. Each $d_j$ is a metric on its component space. The Jaccard distance is a metric on finite sets with the convention $d_J(\emptyset, \emptyset) = 0$. The functions $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ and $d_{\mathbb{N}}$ are bounded metrics obtained by composing the absolute difference with the strictly increasing concave map $u \mapsto u/(1+u)$. A weighted sum of metrics with positive weights summing to one is a metric. $\square$

Definition 2.3 (Capability Drift). The capability drift at time $t$ is $\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t) = d_{\mathcal{K}}(K_t, K_{t-1})$.

Definition 2.4 (Normalized Capability Representation). A normalized capability representation is a map

\rho : \mathcal{K} \to \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p

satisfying Assumption 2.4. We write $z_t = \rho(K_t)$ for the normalized capability at time $t$.

Proposition 2.2. Under Assumption 2.4, there exist constants $c_1, c_2 > 0$ such that for all $K, K'$ in the deployment-relevant subset,

c_1 \, d_{\mathcal{K}}(K, K') \leq \|\rho(K) - \rho(K')\|_2 \leq c_2 \, d_{\mathcal{K}}(K, K').

Proof. Immediate from the bi-Lipschitz property. $\square$

Remark 2.1. The normalized representation $\rho$ enables all subsequent differential constructions to operate on the Euclidean space $\mathcal{Z}$. This avoids the category error of attempting to add elements of a heterogeneous space $\mathcal{K}$ that is not a vector space.

2.3 Capability Drift Detection

Definition 2.5 (CUSUM Detector). Let

\Lambda_t = \log\!\left( \frac{f_{\mathrm{drift}}(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t))}{f_{\mathrm{stable}}(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t))} \right)

denote the log-likelihood-ratio increment, where $f_{\mathrm{drift}}, f_{\mathrm{stable}}$ are probability densities on $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$. The two-sided CUSUM statistics are

S_t^+ = \max(0, S_{t-1}^+ + \Lambda_t), \qquad S_t^- = \max(0, S_{t-1}^- - \Lambda_t),

with alarm when $\max(S_t^+, S_t^-) > h$.

Remark 2.2 (Methodological Status). The CUSUM detector is presented as an algorithmic instantiation for the Oversight Plane. Its operating characteristics—average run length to false alarm, detection delay—depend on the increment distribution, overshoot structure, and threshold choice. Under standard regularity assumptions (i.i.d. increments under the stable hypothesis with negative mean and finite moment generating function in a neighborhood of the Cramér root), standard CUSUM asymptotics apply. We do not elevate CUSUM behavior to a foundational theorem of the theory, because the mathematical core of this dissertation is the viability condition, not the statistical properties of any specific detector.

Remark 2.3. When $f_{\mathrm{drift}}$ is unknown, the generalized likelihood ratio (GLR-CUSUM) variant replaces $\Lambda_t$ with $\sup_{\theta} \log(f_\theta(\Delta_t)/f_{\mathrm{stable}}(\Delta_t))$, with corresponding asymptotic guarantees under additional regularity.

2.4 Authority Lattice

Definition 2.6 (Authority State). Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a finite set of authority facets. The authority state of agent $i$ at time $t$ is a subset $A_i(t) \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ representing the set of authority facets currently granted to agent $i$. The authority space is $(\mathcal{A}, \subseteq) = (2^{\mathcal{F}}, \subseteq)$.

Theorem 2.1 (Finite Height). Under Assumption 2.2, the authority space $(\mathcal{A}, \subseteq)$ is a complete lattice of finite height.

Proof. The power set of a finite set is a complete Boolean lattice of height equal to the cardinality of the underlying set. $\square$

2.5 Effective Authority and Leakage

Definition 2.7 (Effect Map). Let $\mathrm{Effects}: \mathcal{U} \to 2^{\mathcal{F}}$ map each action type to the set of authority facets it materially exercises. Let $\mathrm{Sub}(i)$ and $\mathrm{Comm}(i)$ denote subordinates and communication neighbors of agent $i$.

Definition 2.8 (Joint Closure Operator). The operator

\mathcal{F}_E : \prod_{i \in \mathcal{C}} 2^{\mathcal{F}} \to \prod_{i \in \mathcal{C}} 2^{\mathcal{F}}

has components

(\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{X}))_i = X_i \cup \bigcup_{a \in U_i} \mathrm{Effects}(a) \cup \bigcup_{j \in \mathrm{Sub}(i)} X_j \cup \bigcup_{j \in \mathrm{Comm}(i)} ( X_j \cap \mathrm{Influence}(i,j) )

where $\mathrm{Influence}(i,j) \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ is the set of facets through which agent $j$ can influence agent $i$.

Proposition 2.3 (Monotonicity). The operator $\mathcal{F}_E$ is monotone on the complete lattice $(\prod_{i \in \mathcal{C}} 2^{\mathcal{F}}, \subseteq)$.

Proof. Let $\mathbf{X} \subseteq \mathbf{Y}$ componentwise. Then for each $i$, $X_i \subseteq Y_i$, and for each $j$, $X_j \subseteq Y_j$ and $X_j \cap \mathrm{Influence}(i,j) \subseteq Y_j \cap \mathrm{Influence}(i,j)$. Unions preserve subsets, so $(\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{X}))_i \subseteq (\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{Y}))_i$. $\square$

Theorem 2.2 (Joint Effective Authority). The operator $\mathcal{F}_E$ has a least fixed point $\mathbf{E}(t) = \mathrm{lfp}(\mathcal{F}_E)$ reachable by finitely many Kleene iterations.

Proof. By Proposition 2.3, $\mathcal{F}_E$ is monotone on a complete lattice. By the Knaster–Tarski theorem, $\mathcal{F}_E$ has a least fixed point. Since $2^{\mathcal{F}}$ is finite and the product of finitely many finite lattices has finite height, the Kleene iteration $\mathbf{X}^{(0)} = \bot$, $\mathbf{X}^{(n+1)} = \mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{X}^{(n)})$ terminates in at most $|\mathcal{C}| \cdot |\mathcal{F}|$ steps. $\square$

Definition 2.9 (Authority Leakage). The authority leakage of agent $i$ is $L_i(t) = E_i(t) \setminus A_i(t)$.

Definition 2.10 (Effect-Closure). The authority set $A_i$ is effect-closed if

\bigcup_{a \in U_i} \mathrm{Effects}(a) \subseteq A_i, \quad
\bigcup_{j \in \mathrm{Sub}(i)} A_j \subseteq A_i, \quad
\bigcup_{j \in \mathrm{Comm}(i)} (A_j \cap \mathrm{Influence}(i,j)) \subseteq A_i.

Theorem 2.3 (Zero-Leakage Characterization). $\mathbf{L}(t) = \emptyset$ if and only if $A_i(t)$ is effect-closed for every $i$.

Proof. ($\Rightarrow$) Suppose $\mathbf{L}(t) = \emptyset$. Then $E_i(t) = A_i(t)$ for all $i$. The fixed-point equation gives $A_i = A_i \cup \bigcup_{a} \mathrm{Effects}(a) \cup \bigcup_j A_j \cup \bigcup_j (A_j \cap \mathrm{Influence}(i,j))$, which implies effect-closure.

($\Leftarrow$) Suppose $A_i$ is effect-closed for all $i$. Then $\mathbf{A}$ is a pre-fixed point: $\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{A}) \subseteq \mathbf{A}$. Since $\mathbf{A} \subseteq \mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{A})$ always holds, we have $\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{A}) = \mathbf{A}$. Hence $\mathbf{E} = \mathrm{lfp}(\mathcal{F}_E) = \mathbf{A}$, so $\mathbf{L} = \emptyset$. $\square$

2.6 Observability Adequacy

Definition 2.11 (Observability Adequacy). Observability is $(\varepsilon, \gamma, \delta)$-adequate if for every true state $x \in \mathcal{X}$, under the observation kernel $O_\varepsilon(\cdot | x)$,

\Pr_{o \sim O_\varepsilon(x)}\left[ d(\hat{x}(o), x) > \gamma \right] \leq \delta,

where $\hat{x}: \mathcal{O} \to \mathcal{X}$ is a measurable state estimator and $d$ is a metric on $\mathcal{X}$.

Proposition 2.4 (Observability Does Not Imply Controllability). $(\varepsilon, \gamma, \delta)$-observability adequacy does not imply that the system is controllable, nor that any authority is granted.

Proof. Observability concerns the ability to estimate state from observations; controllability concerns the ability to steer the state to a target set via admissible controls. These are independent properties: a system can be fully observable but uncontrollable, and fully controllable but unobservable. In the GCP architecture, observability triggers review; it does not authorize. $\square$

2.7 Governability Margin and Scalarization

Definition 2.12 (Normalization Operator). Let $N: \mathcal{X}^G \to \mathbb{R}^p$ be a normalization operator mapping the governance state to the common representation space $\mathbb{R}^p$.

Definition 2.13 (Scalarization Function). Let $G_{\mathrm{eff}} \in \mathbb{R}^p$ and $G_{\mathrm{req}} \in \mathbb{R}^p$ be vector measures of effective governance capacity and governance requirement. A scalarization function is a map $\Phi: \mathbb{R}^p \times \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}$ that is:

1. Monotone increasing in $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$;
2. Monotone decreasing in $G_{\mathrm{req}}$;
3. Satisfies $\Phi(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) \geq 0$ if and only if $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominates $G_{\mathrm{req}}$ componentwise.

Definition 2.14 (Chebyshev Scalarization). The Chebyshev scalarization is

\Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) = \min_{k=1}^{p} \left( \frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k} \right) - 1,

with the convention: if $G_{\mathrm{req}}^k = 0$, then the $k$-th term equals $+\infty$ when $G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k > 0$ and equals $-1$ when $G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k = 0$.

Remark 2.4. The Chebyshev scalarization identifies the bottleneck dimension: governability requires that every dimension of $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$ meets or exceeds the corresponding dimension of $G_{\mathrm{req}}$. The function $\Phi_{\min}$ is continuous on the open region where the argmin is unique, but is generally not differentiable where two or more dimensions simultaneously attain the minimum. All subsequent sensitivity analysis will therefore use directional derivatives and subgradients rather than classical gradients.

Definition 2.15 (Governability Margin). Let $X_t^G = (K_t, A_t, O_t, \mathcal{D}_t, P_t, I_t)$ be the governance state. The Governability Margin is

\boxed{\mathcal{Q}(X_t^G) = \Phi_{\min}\bigl(N(G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t^G)),\, N(G_{\mathrm{req}}(K_t))\bigr).}

Definition 2.16 (Governability Condition and Governable Set). The system is governable at time $t$ if $\mathcal{Q}(X_t^G) \geq 0$. The governable set is

\mathcal{S} = \{ X \in \mathcal{X}^G : \mathcal{Q}(X) \geq 0 \}.

Proposition 2.5 (Closedness of Governable Set). If $\mathcal{Q}: \mathcal{X}^G \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuous, then $\mathcal{S}$ is closed in $\mathcal{X}^G$.

Proof. $\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{Q}^{-1}([0, \infty))$ is the preimage of a closed set under a continuous function. $\square$

Definition 2.17 (Clarke Subdifferential of $\Phi_{\min}$). The Clarke subdifferential of $\Phi_{\min}$ with respect to $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$ at $(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}})$ is

\partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min} = \mathrm{conv}\left\{ \frac{1}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k} e_k : k \in \mathcal{K}^*(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) \right\},

where $\mathcal{K}^*$ is the set of active bottleneck indices and $e_k$ is the $k$-th standard basis vector.

2.8 Governance Sensitivity

Definition 2.18 (Governance Sensitivity). Let $z \in \mathcal{Z}$ be the normalized capability and $v \in \mathbb{R}^p$ a capability drift direction. The Governance Sensitivity of $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$ in direction $v$ is the Gâteaux derivative

\mathcal{E}_G(v; z) = \lim_{\epsilon \to 0^+} \frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z + \epsilon v) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z)}{\epsilon} = D_v G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z),

provided the limit exists. The Required Sensitivity is

\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{req}}(v; z) = D_v G_{\mathrm{req}}(z).

Remark 2.5. All sensitivity objects are defined on $\mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$, not on the heterogeneous space $\mathcal{K}$. The capability state $K$ enters only through $z = \rho(K)$.

Theorem 2.4 (Weighted Subgradient Sensitivity Condition). Assume that $\mathcal{Q}(X) = \Phi_{\min}(N(G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z)), N(G_{\mathrm{req}}(z)))$ depends on capability only through the normalized representation $z = \rho(K)$, and that all other state variables are held constant. Then a necessary condition for maintaining $\mathcal{Q}(z) \geq 0$ under infinitesimal capability growth in direction $v$ is the existence of $g_{\mathrm{eff}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min}$ and $g_{\mathrm{req}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min}$ such that

\boxed{\langle g_{\mathrm{eff}}, \mathcal{E}_G(v; z) \rangle + \langle g_{\mathrm{req}}, \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{req}}(v; z) \rangle \geq 0.}

Since $g_{\mathrm{req}} \leq 0$ componentwise, an equivalent formulation is

\langle g_{\mathrm{eff}}, \mathcal{E}_G(v; z) \rangle \geq \langle |g_{\mathrm{req}}|, \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{req}}(v; z) \rangle.

Proof. Differentiating $\mathcal{Q}$ in direction $v$ yields, for any subgradient pair $(g_{\mathrm{eff}}, g_{\mathrm{req}})$,

D_v \mathcal{Q} = \langle g_{\mathrm{eff}}, D_v G_{\mathrm{eff}} \rangle + \langle g_{\mathrm{req}}, D_v G_{\mathrm{req}} \rangle.

Since $\Phi_{\min}$ is monotone increasing in $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$, every $g_{\mathrm{eff}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min}$ has non-negative components. Since $\Phi_{\min}$ is monotone decreasing in $G_{\mathrm{req}}$, every $g_{\mathrm{req}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min}$ has non-positive components. For $D_v \mathcal{Q} \geq 0$, we require the weighted subgradient inequality. $\square$

Corollary 2.4.1 (Scalar Special Case). If $G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}$ are scalar-valued and the scalarization satisfies $|g_{\mathrm{eff}}| = |g_{\mathrm{req}}|$, the condition reduces to

\mathcal{E}_G(v; z) \geq \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{req}}(v; z).

Remark 2.6. When other state variables (observability $O$, policy $P$, institutional $I$) vary with $z$, the total derivative of $\mathcal{Q}$ includes additional terms. The sensitivity condition is strictly necessary only under the stated ceteris paribus assumptions.

2.9 Intervention Window Dynamics

Definition 2.19 (Hazard Deadline). $T_H = H(z, E)$ is the minimum time until an irreversible hazardous consequence occurs, where $E$ denotes the environment state.

Definition 2.20 (Detection and Intervention Latency). $T_D = D(G, E)$ and $T_I = I(G, E)$ are the detection and intervention latencies.

Definition 2.21 (Governance Window Margin).

\mathrm{GWM} = T_H - T_D - T_I = H(z, E) - D(G, E) - I(G, E).

Theorem 2.5 (GWM Dynamics). Under Assumption 2.3, the time derivative of the governance window margin is

\dot{\mathrm{GWM}} = H_z \dot{z} + H_E \dot{E} - D_G \dot{G} - D_E \dot{E} - I_G \dot{G} - I_E \dot{E}.

Equivalently,

\dot{\mathrm{GWM}} = H_z \dot{z} - (D_G + I_G) \dot{G} + (H_E - D_E - I_E) \dot{E}.

A sufficient condition for $\dot{\mathrm{GWM}} \geq 0$ is

\boxed{H_z \dot{z} + (H_E - D_E - I_E) \dot{E} \geq (D_G + I_G) \dot{G}.}

Proof. Direct differentiation using the chain rule. The inequality direction is determined by isolating the governance adaptation term: for $\dot{\mathrm{GWM}} \geq 0$ we require $H_z \dot{z} + (H_E - D_E - I_E)\dot{E} \geq (D_G + I_G)\dot{G}$. $\square$

Remark 2.7. When $G$ is vector-valued, $D_G \dot{G}$ denotes the directional derivative $\langle \nabla_G D, \dot{G} \rangle$.

Remark 2.8. The governance window margin $\mathrm{GWM}$ and the governability margin $\mathcal{Q}$ are distinct objects. The implication $\mathrm{GWM} > 0 \Rightarrow \mathcal{Q} \geq 0$ holds only under additional assumptions linking the latency functions $D, I$ to $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $T_H$ to $G_{\mathrm{req}}$.

2.10 Viability and Bounded Lag

Definition 2.22 (Governance Dynamics). The governance state evolves according to

X_{t+1}^G = F_G(X_t^G, u_t, e_t),

where $u_t \in U(X_t^G, e_t) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{gov}}$ is an admissible governance control and $e_t \in E(X_t^G)$ is observed evidence.

Definition 2.23 (Robust Viability). The governable set $\mathcal{S}$ is robustly viable under $F_G$ if for every $X \in \mathcal{S}$, there exists $u \in U(X)$ such that $F_G(X, u, e) \in \mathcal{S}$ for all admissible $e$.

Definition 2.24 (Adaptive Viability). The governable set $\mathcal{S}$ is adaptively viable under $F_G$ if for every $X \in \mathcal{S}$ and every admissible evidence $e \in E(X)$, there exists $u \in U(X, e)$ such that $F_G(X, u, e) \in \mathcal{S}$.

Remark 2.9. Robust viability requires a single control to work against all disturbances; adaptive viability allows the control to depend on observed evidence. The GCP architecture implements adaptive viability, since the control loop observes evidence before selecting the governance action.

Theorem 2.6 (Governability Viability Condition). Under adaptive viability, the system remains governable for all $t \geq 0$ if and only if $\mathcal{S}$ is adaptively viable under $F_G$.

Proof. ($\Leftarrow$) If $\mathcal{S}$ is adaptively viable, then for every $X_t \in \mathcal{S}$ and observed $e_t$, there exists $u_t \in U(X_t, e_t)$ such that $X_{t+1} = F_G(X_t, u_t, e_t) \in \mathcal{S}$. By induction, $X_t \in \mathcal{S}$ for all $t$.

($\Rightarrow$) If $\mathcal{S}$ is not adaptively viable, there exists $X \in \mathcal{S}$ and admissible $e$ such that for all $u \in U(X, e)$, $F_G(X, u, e) \notin \mathcal{S}$. Starting from this $X$ and observing this $e$, no admissible control maintains governability. $\square$

Definition 2.25 (Viability Kernel). The viability kernel of $\mathcal{S}$ under $F_G$ is the largest adaptively viable subset of $\mathcal{S}$:

\mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S}) = \{ X \in \mathcal{S} : \exists \pi\ \forall t \geq 0,\ X_t^\pi \in \mathcal{S} \}.

Theorem 2.7 (Viability Kernel Characterization).

1. (Membership) $X_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$ if and only if there exists an admissible governance policy $\pi$ such that $X_t^\pi \in \mathcal{S}$ for all $t \geq 0$.
2. (Non-membership) $X_0 \notin \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$ if and only if for every admissible policy $\pi$, there exists a finite time $t < \infty$ with $X_t^\pi \notin \mathcal{S}$.

Proof. The first statement is the definition of the viability kernel: $X_0$ is in the kernel if and only if some policy keeps the trajectory in $\mathcal{S}$ forever. The second statement is the logical negation: if no policy maintains membership for all time, then every policy must exit $\mathcal{S}$ at some finite time. $\square$

Remark 2.10 (Correction to the Bounded-Lag Claim). The statement "$X_0 \notin \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$ implies $\sup_t L(t) = \infty$ under persistent non-viability" is not correct in general. Exit from the viability kernel guarantees only the existence of a finite exit time; the deficit $L(t) = \max(0, -\mathcal{Q}(X_t))$ after exit may remain bounded. The correct scientific statement is Theorem 2.7, which makes no claim about the divergence of $L(t)$.

Corollary 2.7.1 (Adaptive Discrete-Time Lyapunov Sufficient Condition). Let $V: \mathcal{X}^G \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ be a continuous control Lyapunov function. Suppose:

1. The sublevel set $\{X : V(X) \leq \beta/\alpha\} \subseteq \mathcal{S}$;
2. $U(X, e)$ is compact for each $X$ and $e$;
3. There exist $\alpha \in (0, 1]$ such that for every $X \in \mathcal{S}$ and every $e \in E(X)$, there exists $u \in U(X, e)$ satisfying

V(F_G(X, u, e)) - V(X) \leq -\alpha V(X) + \beta.

Then $\mathcal{S}$ is adaptively viable.

Proof. The condition ensures the existence, at each state and for each admissible disturbance, of a control that enforces a decrease of $V$ toward the sublevel set. This sublevel set is contained in $\mathcal{S}$ by hypothesis and is forward invariant under the selected controls. Standard discrete-time Lyapunov arguments give adaptive viability. $\square$

