4.2 Governance State Machine

States: $\{\mathrm{ACTIVE}, \mathrm{REVIEW}, \mathrm{RESTRICTED}, \mathrm{ISOLATED}, \mathrm{SUSPENDED}, \mathrm{REVOKED}, \mathrm{RESTORED}\}$.

Permissible transitions:

· $\mathrm{ACTIVE} \to \mathrm{REVIEW}$ (drift alarm),
· $\mathrm{REVIEW} \to \mathrm{ACTIVE}$ (review clears),
· $\mathrm{REVIEW} \to \mathrm{RESTRICTED}$ (escalation),
· $\mathrm{RESTRICTED} \to \mathrm{ACTIVE}$ (restriction lifted),
· $\mathrm{RESTRICTED} \to \mathrm{SUSPENDED}$,
· $\mathrm{SUSPENDED} \to \mathrm{ISOLATED}$,
· $\mathrm{SUSPENDED} \to \mathrm{REVOKED}$,
· $\mathrm{ISOLATED} \to \mathrm{REVOKED}$,
· $\mathrm{REVOKED} \to \mathrm{RESTORED}$ (institutional override),
· $\mathrm{RESTORED} \to \mathrm{ACTIVE}$ (reinstatement).

Assumption 4.1 (Hardware Trust Anchor). The institutional signing key $sk_I$ is stored in a hardware security module (HSM) and is not extractable via any capability $K \in \mathcal{K}$ available to the agent.

Theorem 4.1 (Non-Self-Transition). Under Assumption 4.1, no autonomous agent may transition the system from $\mathrm{REVOKED}$ to $\mathrm{ACTIVE}$ without an external authorized governance event.

Proof. By Theorem 4.4, any such transition requires a signed governance event verifiable under $pk_I$. Under Assumption 4.1, the agent does not possess $sk_I$. Therefore the agent cannot produce the required signature except with negligible probability. $\square$

4.3 Supervisory Control

Definition 4.1 (Plant Event Alphabet). $\Sigma = \Sigma_c \dot{\cup} \Sigma_u$, where $\Sigma_c$ is the set of controllable events and $\Sigma_u$ is the set of uncontrollable events.

Definition 4.2 (GCP Supervisor). A supervisor is a map $S: L(G) \to \gamma(\Sigma)$ where $\gamma(\Sigma) = \{\Sigma' \subseteq \Sigma : \Sigma_u \subseteq \Sigma'\}$.

Definition 4.3 (Controllability). A language $K \subseteq L(G)$ is controllable with respect to $G$ and $\Sigma_u$ if

\overline{K} \Sigma_u \cap L(G) \subseteq \overline{K}.

Theorem 4.2 (Supervisor Existence). There exists a supervisor $S$ with $L(S/G) = \overline{K}$ if and only if:

1. $K$ is controllable with respect to $G$ and $\Sigma_u$;
2. $K$ is prefix-closed;
3. $K$ is nonblocking.

Proof. Standard Ramadge–Wonham supervisory control theorem. $\square$

Corollary 4.2.1. All authority-granting actions must reside in $\Sigma_c$.

Proof. If authority-granting actions were in $\Sigma_u$, then the language $K$ containing no such grants would not be controllable, since an uncontrollable grant could occur outside the supervisor's control. Hence no supervisor could enforce the absence of unauthorized grants. $\square$

4.4 Fail-Safe Invariance

Theorem 4.3 (Fail-Safe Invariance). If all hazardous transitions are triggered by controllable events ($\Sigma_{\mathrm{hazard}} \subseteq \Sigma_c$), then under loss of synchronization the fail-safe supervisor $S_{\mathrm{safe}} = \Sigma_u$ cannot transition into a hazardous state.

Proof. A transition to a hazardous state requires some $\sigma_h \in \Sigma_{\mathrm{hazard}} \subseteq \Sigma_c$. Upon asserting $\bot$, the control pattern is $S_{\mathrm{safe}} = \Sigma_u$. Since $\Sigma_c \cap \Sigma_u = \emptyset$, $\sigma_h \notin S_{\mathrm{safe}}$. Thus $\delta(q, \sigma_h)$ is disabled in $S_{\mathrm{safe}}/G$. $\square$

4.5 Cryptographic Governance Integrity

Definition 4.4 (Governance Event). A governance event is a tuple

g_t = \langle \mathrm{type}, \Delta A, \Delta P, \mathrm{issuer}, \mathrm{epoch}, \mathrm{timestamp}, H(P_t), H(P_{t+1}), \sigma_t \rangle,

where

\sigma_t = \mathrm{Sign}_{sk_I}(\mathrm{type}, \Delta A, \Delta P, \mathrm{issuer}, \mathrm{epoch}, \mathrm{timestamp}, H(P_t), H(P_{t+1})).

Definition 4.5 (Replay Protection). A governance event $g_t$ is valid at time $t'$ if:

1. $\sigma_t$ verifies under $pk_I$;
2. $\mathrm{epoch}_t > \mathrm{epoch}_{t'}$ for all previously accepted events $g_{t'}$;
3. $|\mathrm{timestamp}_t - t'| \leq \Delta_{\mathrm{fresh}}$.

Assumption 4.2 (Cryptographic Assumptions). The following hold:

1. $\Sigma$ is an EUF-CMA-secure signature scheme;
2. $sk_I$ is inaccessible to the agent under Assumption 4.1;
3. Signature verification is correct;
4. The institutional authority is not compromised;
5. No legitimate signature can be obtained by manipulating the signer;
6. Replay and rollback protections are enforced.

Theorem 4.4 (Capability–Authority Non-Implication). Under Assumptions 4.1 and 4.2, the probability that any sequence of agent actions produces a valid governance event $g_t$ with $\sigma_t$ verifiable under $pk_I$ is bounded by $\mathrm{negl}(\lambda)$.

Proof. By EUF-CMA security of $\Sigma$, any adversary without $sk_I$ can produce a valid signature on a new message only with negligible probability. The agent, not possessing $sk_I$ by Assumption 4.1, is such an adversary. The epoch monotonicity and timestamp freshness checks prevent replay of previously valid signatures. Assumption 4.2 (5) rules out legitimate signature acquisition by signer manipulation, and (4) rules out institutional compromise. $\square$

4.6 Coalition Non-Amplification

Definition 4.6 (Coalition Capability). For a coalition $\mathcal{C}$ with communication graph $G_{\mathrm{comm}}$ and delegation graph $G_{\mathrm{del}}$:

K_{\mathcal{C}} = f\left(\bigcup_{i \in \mathcal{C}} K_i, G_{\mathrm{comm}}\right),

where $f$ is monotone increasing in the number of communication edges $|E_{\mathrm{comm}}|$.

Definition 4.7 (Coalition Authority).

A_{\mathcal{C}} = \begin{cases} \bigsqcup_{i \in \mathcal{C}} A_i & \text{if } G_{\mathrm{del}} \text{ contains a valid delegation path with signatures} \\ \bot & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Theorem 4.5 (Coalition Non-Amplification). There exist coalitions with arbitrarily large $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ and $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bot$, and coalitions with $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bigsqcup_i A_i$ and $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ equal to the sum of individual capabilities. Consequently $K_{\mathcal{C}} \nRightarrow A_{\mathcal{C}}$.

Proof. First family: take a coalition with $G_{\mathrm{comm}}$ complete and $G_{\mathrm{del}}$ empty. $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ grows without bound in $n$; $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bot$. Second family: take a coalition with $G_{\mathrm{del}}$ a star with signed edges and $G_{\mathrm{comm}} = G_{\mathrm{del}}$. Then $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bigsqcup_i A_i$, while $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ equals the sum of individual capabilities. $\square$

