6. Conclusion

The first thesis establishes

\text{Agent Intelligence} \neq \text{Agent Authority}.

The present dissertation extends the separation:

\text{Agent Authority} \neq \text{Institutional Governance}.

The resulting hierarchy is

\text{Intelligence} \to \text{Capability} \to \text{Authority} \to \text{Execution},

while the governance relationship is

\text{Institution} \to \text{Governance} \to \text{Authority} \to \text{Control} \to \text{Execution}.

The fundamental requirement is that the second chain remains external to the first, and that the second chain maintains a non-negative Governability Margin:

\boxed{\mathcal{Q}(X_t) \geq 0.}

More precisely, the system must satisfy the viability condition: the governable set $\mathcal{S} = \{X : \mathcal{Q}(X) \geq 0\}$ must be adaptively viable under the governance dynamics $F_G$ with admissible controls $U(X, e)$.

Governability is not an instantaneous property; it is the existence of an admissible governance policy capable of maintaining the evolving system inside the governable set:

\boxed{X_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S}) \iff \exists \pi \ \forall t \geq 0,\ X_t^\pi \in \mathcal{S}.}

The purpose of the Governance Control Plane is not to control intelligence itself. It is to ensure that

\text{Increasing Intelligence} \nRightarrow \text{Increasing Sovereignty}

and that autonomous systems remain

\text{Capable} \;\wedge\; \text{Observable} \;\wedge\; \text{Bounded} \;\wedge\; \text{Revocable} \;\wedge\; \text{Accountable},

while preserving meaningful human and institutional authority.

The two theses in relation:

\boxed{\textbf{THESIS I: AGENT CONTROL PLANE}}

Governing what an agent may execute.

\Downarrow

\boxed{\textbf{THESIS II: GOVERNANCE CONTROL PLANE}}

Governing who may define, delegate, observe, modify, and revoke that authority—and ensuring that the Governability Margin $\mathcal{Q}(X_t)$ remains non-negative through the adaptive viability of the governable set $\mathcal{S}$ under the governance dynamics.

Together:

\text{Intelligence} \neq \text{Capability} \neq \text{Authority} \neq \text{Governance}.

This four-way separation constitutes the conceptual foundation of a formal architecture for governable autonomous intelligence. It is offered as a theoretical specification, with its theorems stated and proved, its assumptions explicit, its architectural boundaries named, and its residual risks—including the political problem of institutional legitimacy—left where they belong: outside the cryptographic boundary, and inside the human one.

