Appendix A: Normalized Capability Representation

A.1 Motivation

The capability space

\mathcal{K} = 2^{\mathcal{T}} \times 2^{\mathcal{R}} \times \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \times 2^{\mathcal{I}} \times 2^{\mathcal{U}} \times \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \times 2^{\mathcal{M}}

is a heterogeneous product of finite power sets, non-negative reals, and natural numbers. It is not a vector space: the operations $K + \epsilon v$ and $\epsilon \to 0$ are undefined for elements of $\mathcal{K}$ that include subsets and integers. All differential constructions in the theory therefore require a normalized Euclidean representation.

A.2 Construction

Definition A.1 (Normalized Capability Representation). Let the primitive sets $\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{M}$ be finite with cardinalities $|\mathcal{T}|, |\mathcal{R}|, |\mathcal{I}|, |\mathcal{U}|, |\mathcal{M}|$. Define

\rho: \mathcal{K} \to [0, 1]^8 \subseteq \mathbb{R}^8

by

\rho(K) = \left( \frac{|T|}{|\mathcal{T}|}, \frac{|R|}{|\mathcal{R}|}, \frac{B}{1+B}, \frac{|I|}{|\mathcal{I}|}, \frac{|U|}{|\mathcal{U}|}, \frac{H}{1+H}, \frac{N}{1+N}, \frac{|M|}{|\mathcal{M}|} \right).

We write $z = \rho(K) \in \mathcal{Z} := \rho(\mathcal{K}) \subseteq [0, 1]^8$ for the normalized capability.

Proposition A.1 (Well-Definedness). The map $\rho$ is well-defined on $\mathcal{K}$ and takes values in $[0, 1]^8$.

Proof. Each component is either a ratio of a cardinality to a fixed finite bound (hence in $[0, 1]$) or a normalized quantity of the form $u/(1+u)$ with $u \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ or $u \in \mathbb{N}$ (hence in $[0, 1)$). $\square$

Proposition A.2 (Continuity). The map $\rho$ is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the capability metric $d_{\mathcal{K}}$ and the Euclidean metric on $\mathbb{R}^8$.

Proof. Consider each component. For the tool coordinate, if $d_J(T, T') < \delta$, then

\left| \frac{|T|}{|\mathcal{T}|} - \frac{|T'|}{|\mathcal{T}|} \right| = \frac{||T| - |T'||}{|\mathcal{T}|} \leq \frac{|T \triangle T'|}{|\mathcal{T}|} \leq d_J(T, T').

The last inequality follows from the definition of Jaccard distance: $d_J(T, T') = |T \triangle T'| / |T \cup T'| \geq |T \triangle T'| / |\mathcal{T}|$. The normalized budget component satisfies

\left| \frac{B}{1+B} - \frac{B'}{1+B'} \right| = \frac{|B - B'|}{(1+B)(1+B')} \leq |B - B'|.

The normalized horizon and replication components satisfy analogous bounds. Summing over all eight components and applying the weighted structure of $d_{\mathcal{K}}$ gives the Lipschitz property. $\square$

Proposition A.3 (Finiteness of Lipschitz Constant). The Lipschitz constant

L_\rho = \sup_{K \neq K'} \frac{\|\rho(K) - \rho(K')\|_2}{d_{\mathcal{K}}(K, K')}

is finite.

Proof. Each component of $\rho$ is bounded in $[0, 1]$, so $\|\rho(K) - \rho(K')\|_2 \leq \sqrt{8}$. The capability metric satisfies $d_{\mathcal{K}} \geq \min_j w_j \cdot d_j$ for the component with the largest contribution. Hence $L_\rho \leq \sqrt{8} / \min_j w_j < \infty$. $\square$

A.3 Extension to a Neighborhood

Assumption A.1 (Neighborhood Extension). There exist an open set $\mathcal{U} \supseteq \mathcal{Z}$ in $\mathbb{R}^p$ (with $p = 8$) and continuously differentiable extensions $\tilde{G}_{\mathrm{eff}}, \tilde{G}_{\mathrm{req}}: \mathcal{U} \to \mathbb{R}^q$ of $G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}$.

Remark A.1. Under Assumption A.1, the Gâteaux derivative $D_v G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z)$ is well-defined for all $z \in \mathcal{Z}$ and all $v \in \mathbb{R}^p$ such that $z + \epsilon v \in \mathcal{U}$ for sufficiently small $\epsilon > 0$.

Remark A.2 (Injectivity). The map $\rho$ is not injective: two distinct tool sets with equal cardinality map to the same point. This is a deliberate modeling choice: the normalized representation captures the scale of the capability state, not its exact identity. When exact identity is required, one may use the indicator embedding

\rho_{\mathrm{ind}}(K) = \left( \frac{1_T}{\sqrt{|\mathcal{T}|}}, \frac{1_R}{\sqrt{|\mathcal{R}|}}, \frac{B}{1+B}, \frac{1_I}{\sqrt{|\mathcal{I}|}}, \frac{1_U}{\sqrt{|\mathcal{U}|}}, \frac{H}{1+H}, \frac{N}{1+N}, \frac{1_M}{\sqrt{|\mathcal{M}|}} \right),

where $1_T \in \{0, 1\}^{|\mathcal{T}|}$ is the indicator vector of $T$. The indicator embedding is injective but high-dimensional. The theory requires only Lipschitz continuity of $\rho$ and differentiability of $G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}$ on a neighborhood of $\mathcal{Z}$.

