Appendix B: Subdifferential Calculus for Chebyshev Scalarization

B.1 Definition and Elementary Properties

Definition B.1 (Chebyshev Scalarization). For $G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}} \in \mathbb{R}^p$ with $G_{\mathrm{req}}^k > 0$ for all $k$, define

\Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) = \min_{k=1}^{p} \left( \frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k} \right) - 1.

Proposition B.1 (Monotonicity). $\Phi_{\min}$ is monotone increasing in $G_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and monotone decreasing in $G_{\mathrm{req}}$.

Proof. Let $G_{\mathrm{eff}} \leq G_{\mathrm{eff}}'$ componentwise. Then for each $k$, $G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k / G_{\mathrm{req}}^k \leq (G_{\mathrm{eff}}')^k / G_{\mathrm{req}}^k$, so the minimum over $k$ is monotone. Similarly, if $G_{\mathrm{req}} \leq G_{\mathrm{req}}'$ componentwise, then $G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k / G_{\mathrm{req}}^k \geq G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k / (G_{\mathrm{req}}')^k$, so the minimum is monotone decreasing. $\square$

Proposition B.2 (Dominance Characterization). $\Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) \geq 0$ if and only if $G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k \geq G_{\mathrm{req}}^k$ for all $k$.

Proof. The minimum of the ratios is $\geq 1$ if and only if every ratio is $\geq 1$. $\square$

B.2 Non-Differentiability at Bottleneck Ties

Proposition B.3 (Non-Differentiability). Let $\mathcal{K}^*(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) = \{k : G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k / G_{\mathrm{req}}^k = \min_j G_{\mathrm{eff}}^j / G_{\mathrm{req}}^j\}$ denote the set of active bottleneck indices. If $|\mathcal{K}^*| \geq 2$, then $\Phi_{\min}$ is not differentiable at $(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}})$.

Proof. When $|\mathcal{K}^*| \geq 2$, the minimum function coincides with different smooth functions $r_k = G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k / G_{\mathrm{req}}^k$ on different sides of the point. Their gradients differ, so the classical gradient does not exist. $\square$

Example B.1. Let $p = 2$, $G_{\mathrm{req}} = (1, 1)$, $G_{\mathrm{eff}} = (1, 1)$. Then $\Phi_{\min} = 0$. The directional derivative depends on the perturbation direction: perturbing $G_{\mathrm{eff}} = (1 + \epsilon, 1)$ gives $\Phi_{\min} = \epsilon$; perturbing $G_{\mathrm{eff}} = (1, 1 + \epsilon)$ also gives $\Phi_{\min} = \epsilon$; but perturbing $G_{\mathrm{eff}} = (1 + \epsilon, 1 - \epsilon)$ gives $\Phi_{\min} = -\epsilon$.

B.3 Clarke Subdifferential

Definition B.2 (Clarke Subdifferential). Let $f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ be locally Lipschitz. The Clarke subdifferential of $f$ at $x$ is

\partial f(x) = \mathrm{conv}\left\{ \lim_{i \to \infty} \nabla f(x_i) : x_i \to x, \ f \text{ differentiable at } x_i \right\}.

Theorem B.1 (Subdifferential of Chebyshev Scalarization). Let $\mathcal{K}^*$ be the set of active bottleneck indices. Then

\partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) = \mathrm{conv}\left\{ \frac{1}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k} e_k : k \in \mathcal{K}^* \right\},

and

\partial_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) = \mathrm{conv}\left\{ -\frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{(G_{\mathrm{req}}^k)^2} e_k : k \in \mathcal{K}^* \right\}.

Proof. In a neighborhood of $(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}})$ where the active set $\mathcal{K}^*$ is constant, the minimum function coincides with $r_k$ for any $k \in \mathcal{K}^*$. Hence

\nabla_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min} = \frac{1}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k} e_k, \qquad \nabla_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min} = -\frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{(G_{\mathrm{req}}^k)^2} e_k

for that $k$. The Clarke subdifferential is the convex hull of all such limits as the differentiable points approach $(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}})$ from all directions. $\square$

Corollary B.1.1 (Sign of Subgradients). Every $g_{\mathrm{eff}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min}$ has non-negative components. Every $g_{\mathrm{req}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min}$ has non-positive components.

B.4 Directional Derivative

Definition B.3 (One-Sided Directional Derivative). The one-sided directional derivative of $\Phi_{\min}$ at $(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}})$ in direction $(v_{\mathrm{eff}}, v_{\mathrm{req}}) \in \mathbb{R}^p \times \mathbb{R}^p$ is

D_{(v_{\mathrm{eff}}, v_{\mathrm{req}})} \Phi_{\min} = \lim_{\epsilon \to 0^+} \frac{\Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}} + \epsilon v_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}} + \epsilon v_{\mathrm{req}}) - \Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}})}{\epsilon}.

Proposition B.4 (Directional Derivative Formula). With active set $\mathcal{K}^*$,

D_{(v_{\mathrm{eff}}, v_{\mathrm{req}})} \Phi_{\min} = \min_{k \in \mathcal{K}^*} \left( \frac{v_{\mathrm{eff}}^k G_{\mathrm{req}}^k - G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k v_{\mathrm{req}}^k}{(G_{\mathrm{req}}^k)^2} \right).

Proof. For small $\epsilon > 0$, expand each ratio:

\frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k + \epsilon v_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k + \epsilon v_{\mathrm{req}}^k} = \frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k} + \epsilon \cdot \frac{v_{\mathrm{eff}}^k G_{\mathrm{req}}^k - G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k v_{\mathrm{req}}^k}{(G_{\mathrm{req}}^k)^2} + O(\epsilon^2).

The minimum over $k$ of the perturbed ratios, after subtracting the minimum of the unperturbed ratios, is asymptotically $\epsilon$ times the minimum over active indices of the coefficient. $\square$

Remark B.1. The directional derivative is the support function of the Clarke subdifferential:

D_{(v_{\mathrm{eff}}, v_{\mathrm{req}})} \Phi_{\min} = \min_{g_{\mathrm{eff}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min}, \ g_{\mathrm{req}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min}} \left[ \langle g_{\mathrm{eff}}, v_{\mathrm{eff}} \rangle + \langle g_{\mathrm{req}}, v_{\mathrm{req}} \rangle \right].

B.5 Smooth Approximation

Definition B.4 (Soft-Min Approximation). For temperature parameter $\tau > 0$, define

\Phi_\tau(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) = -\tau \log \sum_{k=1}^{p} \exp\left( -\frac{r_k}{\tau} \right) - 1, \qquad r_k = \frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k}.

Proposition B.5 (Approximation Property). As $\tau \to 0^+$,

\min_k r_k - \tau \log p \leq -\tau \log \sum_k e^{-r_k/\tau} \leq \min_k r_k.

Hence $\Phi_\tau \to \Phi_{\min}$ uniformly on compact subsets of $\mathbb{R}^p \times \mathbb{R}^p$ with error bounded by $\tau \log p$.

Proof. The lower bound follows from $\sum_k e^{-r_k/\tau} \leq p \cdot e^{-\min_k r_k/\tau}$. The upper bound follows from $\sum_k e^{-r_k/\tau} \geq e^{-\min_k r_k/\tau}$. $\square$

Proposition B.6 (Differentiability). For every $\tau > 0$, $\Phi_\tau$ is continuously differentiable with

\frac{\partial \Phi_\tau}{\partial G_{\mathrm{eff}}^j} = \frac{1}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^j} \cdot \frac{\exp(-r_j/\tau)}{\sum_k \exp(-r_k/\tau)}, \qquad
\frac{\partial \Phi_\tau}{\partial G_{\mathrm{req}}^j} = -\frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^j}{(G_{\mathrm{req}}^j)^2} \cdot \frac{\exp(-r_j/\tau)}{\sum_k \exp(-r_k/\tau)}.

Proof. Direct differentiation of the soft-min function. $\square$

B.6 Subgradient Inequality for Governability

Theorem B.2 (Chain Rule for Governability Sensitivity). Let $\mathcal{Q}(z) = \Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z), G_{\mathrm{req}}(z))$ with $G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}$ continuously differentiable on $\mathcal{Z}$. Then for every $z \in \mathcal{Z}$ and every direction $v \in \mathbb{R}^p$,

D_v \mathcal{Q}(z) = \min_{g_{\mathrm{eff}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min}, \ g_{\mathrm{req}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min}} \left[ \langle g_{\mathrm{eff}}, D_v G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z) \rangle + \langle g_{\mathrm{req}}, D_v G_{\mathrm{req}}(z) \rangle \right].

Proof. By the chain rule for Clarke subdifferentials applied to the composition $\Phi_{\min} \circ (G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}})$, combined with the support function characterization of the directional derivative (Remark B.1). $\square$

Corollary B.2.1 (Necessary Condition for Governability Maintenance). A necessary condition for $D_v \mathcal{Q}(z) \geq 0$ is the existence of $g_{\mathrm{eff}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{eff}}} \Phi_{\min}$ and $g_{\mathrm{req}} \in \partial_{G_{\mathrm{req}}} \Phi_{\min}$ such that

\langle g_{\mathrm{eff}}, D_v G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z) \rangle + \langle g_{\mathrm{req}}, D_v G_{\mathrm{req}}(z) \rangle \geq 0.

Since $g_{\mathrm{req}} \leq 0$ componentwise, an equivalent formulation is

\langle g_{\mathrm{eff}}, D_v G_{\mathrm{eff}}(z) \rangle \geq \langle |g_{\mathrm{req}}|, D_v G_{\mathrm{req}}(z) \rangle.

