Appendix C: Capability Drift Detection

C.1 CUSUM Detector

Definition C.1 (CUSUM Statistic). Let $\{\Lambda_t\}_{t \geq 1}$ be a sequence of log-likelihood-ratio increments

\Lambda_t = \log \frac{f_{\mathrm{drift}}(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t))}{f_{\mathrm{stable}}(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t))},

where $f_{\mathrm{drift}}, f_{\mathrm{stable}}$ are probability densities on $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$. The two-sided CUSUM statistics are

S_t^+ = \max(0, S_{t-1}^+ + \Lambda_t), \qquad S_t^- = \max(0, S_{t-1}^- - \Lambda_t),

with alarm when $\max(S_t^+, S_t^-) > h$.

Assumption C.1 (Regularity). Under the stable hypothesis:

1. $\{\Lambda_t\}$ are i.i.d.;
2. $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[\Lambda_t] < 0$;
3. The moment generating function $M(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[e^{\theta \Lambda_t}]$ exists in a neighborhood of some $\theta^* > 0$;
4. $\theta^*$ is the unique positive solution of $M(\theta^*) = 1$.

C.2 Asymptotic Guarantees

Theorem C.1 (Cramér–Lundberg Bound). Under Assumption C.1,

\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[\tau_{\mathrm{FA}}] \geq e^{\theta^* h},

where $\tau_{\mathrm{FA}}$ is the time to false alarm.

Proof. Consider the exponential martingale $M_t = e^{\theta^* S_t^+ - t \psi(\theta^*)}$ where $\psi(\theta) = \log M(\theta)$. Under the stable hypothesis, $\psi(\theta^*) = 0$, so $M_t = e^{\theta^* S_t^+}$ is a martingale. Optional stopping at $\tau_{\mathrm{FA}} \wedge n$ gives

\mathbb{E}[e^{\theta^* S_{\tau \wedge n}^+}] = 1.

Since $S_{\tau}^+ > h$ at the stopping time, $e^{\theta^* h} \leq \mathbb{E}[e^{\theta^* S_{\tau \wedge n}^+}] \leq \mathbb{E}[e^{\theta^* S_{\tau}^+}]$. Taking $n \to \infty$ and applying Fatou's lemma gives the bound. $\square$

Theorem C.2 (Asymptotic Average Run Length). Under additional regularity conditions on the ladder height distribution,

\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[\tau_{\mathrm{FA}}] = \frac{e^{\theta^* h}}{c + o(1)}, \qquad h \to \infty,

where $c > 0$ is the stationary overshoot coefficient.

Proof. By renewal-theoretic analysis of the overshoot distribution at level $h$. The overshoot $S_{\tau_{\mathrm{FA}}}^+ - h$ converges in distribution to a limiting random variable whose expectation determines $c$. $\square$

C.3 Methodological Status

Remark C.1. The CUSUM detector is an algorithmic component of the Oversight Plane. Its role is to trigger review when capability drift exceeds statistically significant thresholds. The mathematical core of the theory—the viability condition and the viability kernel characterization—does not depend on the specific statistical properties of the drift detector.

Remark C.2 (Alternative Detectors). Other detectors may be substituted without altering the theoretical structure: Shewhart charts for large shifts, EWMA charts for gradual drift, Bayesian change-point models for uncertainty quantification, or domain-specific monitors for structured anomalies.

C.4 GLR-CUSUM Variant

Definition C.2 (Generalized Likelihood Ratio). When $f_{\mathrm{drift}}$ is unknown, the GLR-CUSUM increment is

\Lambda_t^{\mathrm{GLR}} = \sup_{\theta \in \Theta} \log \frac{f_\theta(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t))}{f_{\mathrm{stable}}(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t))}.

Theorem C.3 (GLR-CUSUM Asymptotics). Under regularity conditions on the parametric family $\{f_\theta\}_{\theta \in \Theta}$, the GLR-CUSUM detector achieves the asymptotic ARL exponent $e^{\theta^* h}$ up to a polynomial factor in $h$.

Proof. By the standard theory of generalized likelihood ratio tests for change-point detection, the GLR statistic converges in distribution to a functional of Brownian motion under the null, and to a shifted version under the alternative. The exponential rate of the ARL is preserved. $\square$

