Appendix D: Viability Theory

D.1 Set-Valued Maps and Marchaud Conditions

Definition D.1 (Set-Valued Map). A set-valued map $F: X \rightrightarrows Y$ associates to each $x \in X$ a subset $F(x) \subseteq Y$.

Definition D.2 (Upper Semicontinuity). A set-valued map $F: X \rightrightarrows Y$ between metric spaces is upper semicontinuous at $x_0$ if for every open set $V \supseteq F(x_0)$, there exists a neighborhood $U$ of $x_0$ such that $F(x) \subseteq V$ for all $x \in U$.

Definition D.3 (Marchaud Map). A set-valued map $F: X \rightrightarrows \mathbb{R}^n$ is a Marchaud map if:

1. $F$ is upper semicontinuous;
2. $F(x)$ is non-empty, compact, and convex for all $x$;
3. There exists $c > 0$ such that $\sup_{y \in F(x)} \|y\| \leq c(1 + \|x\|)$ for all $x$.

Assumption D.1 (Marchaud Governance Dynamics). The set-valued map $X \mapsto F_G(X, U(X), \cdot)$ is a Marchaud map.

D.2 Viability Kernel

Definition D.4 (Adaptive Viability). Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{X}^G$ be closed. The set $\mathcal{S}$ is adaptively viable under $F_G$ if for every $X \in \mathcal{S}$ and every $e \in E(X)$, there exists $u \in U(X, e)$ such that $F_G(X, u, e) \in \mathcal{S}$.

Definition D.5 (Viability Kernel). The viability kernel of $\mathcal{S}$ under $F_G$ is the largest adaptively viable subset of $\mathcal{S}$:

\mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S}) = \{ X \in \mathcal{S} : \exists \pi \ \forall t \geq 0, \ X_t^\pi \in \mathcal{S} \}.

Theorem D.1 (Existence of Viability Kernel). Under Assumption D.1 and closedness of $\mathcal{S}$, the viability kernel $\mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$ exists and is closed.

Proof. The union of viable subsets is viable: if $\{\mathcal{V}_\alpha\}$ is a family of viable subsets, then for $X \in \bigcup_\alpha \mathcal{V}_\alpha$ and $e \in E(X)$, there exists a policy keeping the trajectory in one of the $\mathcal{V}_\alpha$ (hence in the union). The closure of a viable set is viable under upper semicontinuity of $F_G$: if $X_n \to X$ with $X_n \in \mathcal{V}$ and $X_n$ viable, then for each $e$, the controls $u_n$ have a convergent subsequence (by compactness of $U(X, e)$) whose limit $u$ satisfies $F_G(X, u, e) \in \overline{\mathcal{V}}$. Hence the maximal viable subset exists and is closed. $\square$

Theorem D.2 (Fixed-Point Characterization). The viability kernel satisfies the fixed-point equation

\mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S}) = \{ X \in \mathcal{S} : \forall e \in E(X), \ \exists u \in U(X, e), \ F_G(X, u, e) \in \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S}) \}.

Proof. Let $\mathcal{V} = \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$. We show both inclusions.

$(\subseteq)$ If $X \in \mathcal{V}$, then there exists a policy $\pi$ with $X_t^\pi \in \mathcal{S}$ for all $t$. At $t = 0$, for the observed $e_0$, the policy selects $u_0 \in U(X, e_0)$ with $F_G(X, u_0, e_0) \in \mathcal{S}$. Moreover, $F_G(X, u_0, e_0)$ admits the policy $\pi$ shifted by one step, so $F_G(X, u_0, e_0) \in \mathcal{V}$.

$(\supseteq)$ If $X$ satisfies the right-hand side, then for each $e$ we can select $u(e)$ with $F_G(X, u(e), e) \in \mathcal{V}$. Define a policy $\pi$ that at each step selects the appropriate $u$. By induction, the trajectory remains in $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{S}$. Hence $X \in \mathcal{V}$. $\square$

Corollary D.2.1 (Membership Characterization).

X_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S}) \iff \exists \pi \ \forall t \geq 0, \ X_t^\pi \in \mathcal{S}.

D.3 Governability Viability Condition

Theorem D.3 (Governability Viability Condition). Under adaptive viability, the system remains governable for all $t \geq 0$ if and only if $\mathcal{S}$ is adaptively viable under $F_G$.

Proof. $(\Leftarrow)$ If $\mathcal{S}$ is adaptively viable, then for every $X_t \in \mathcal{S}$ and observed $e_t$, there exists $u_t \in U(X_t, e_t)$ such that $X_{t+1} = F_G(X_t, u_t, e_t) \in \mathcal{S}$. By induction, $X_t \in \mathcal{S}$ for all $t$.

$(\Rightarrow)$ If $\mathcal{S}$ is not adaptively viable, there exists $X \in \mathcal{S}$ and admissible $e \in E(X)$ such that for all $u \in U(X, e)$, $F_G(X, u, e) \notin \mathcal{S}$. Starting from this $X$ and observing this $e$, no admissible control maintains governability. $\square$

D.4 Bounded Lag

Definition D.6 (Governance Lag). The governance lag at time $t$ is $L(t) = \max(0, -\mathcal{Q}(X_t))$.

Proposition D.1 (Non-Membership Implies Exit). If $X_0 \notin \mathrm{Viab}_{F_G}(\mathcal{S})$, then for every admissible policy $\pi$, there exists a finite time $t < \infty$ with $X_t^\pi \notin \mathcal{S}$.

Proof. Immediate from the definition of the viability kernel: if $X_0$ is not in the kernel, no policy maintains membership in $\mathcal{S}$ for all time. $\square$

Remark D.1. Non-membership in the viability kernel guarantees only the existence of a finite exit time. It does not imply that the lag $L(t)$ diverges. A trajectory may exit $\mathcal{S}$ and subsequently remain in a region with bounded negative margin.

Proposition D.2 (Bounded Lag Sufficient Condition). If there exists $\epsilon > 0$ such that $\mathcal{Q}(X) \leq -\epsilon$ implies $F_G(X, u, e) \in \mathcal{S}$ for some $u \in U(X, e)$ and all $e$, then $L(t)$ is bounded by $\epsilon$.

Proof. After exit from $\mathcal{S}$, the trajectory must satisfy $\mathcal{Q} \in (-\epsilon, 0)$, so $L(t) < \epsilon$. $\square$

