Appendix G: Supervisory Control

G.1 Plant and Supervisor

Definition G.1 (Plant). A plant is a generator $G = (Q, \Sigma, \delta, q_0, Q_m)$ where $Q$ is the state set, $\Sigma$ is the event alphabet, $\delta: Q \times \Sigma \to Q$ is the partial transition function, $q_0$ is the initial state, and $Q_m$ is the set of marked states.

Definition G.2 (Event Partition). The event alphabet is partitioned as $\Sigma = \Sigma_c \dot{\cup} \Sigma_u$, where $\Sigma_c$ is the set of controllable events and $\Sigma_u$ is the set of uncontrollable events.

Definition G.3 (Language). $L(G)$ denotes the language generated by $G$, and $\overline{L(G)}$ denotes its prefix closure.

Definition G.4 (Supervisor). A supervisor is a map $S: L(G) \to \gamma(\Sigma)$ where

\gamma(\Sigma) = \{\Sigma' \subseteq \Sigma : \Sigma_u \subseteq \Sigma'\}.

The supervised system $S/G$ is the plant $G$ under the control pattern imposed by $S$.

G.2 Controllability and Existence

Definition G.5 (Controllability). A language $K \subseteq L(G)$ is controllable with respect to $G$ and $\Sigma_u$ if

\overline{K} \Sigma_u \cap L(G) \subseteq \overline{K}.

Definition G.6 (Prefix-Closure). A language $K$ is prefix-closed if $K = \overline{K}$.

Definition G.7 (Nonblocking). A supervisor $S$ is nonblocking if $L(S/G) = \overline{L_m(S/G)}$.

Theorem G.1 (Supervisor Existence). There exists a supervisor $S$ with $L(S/G) = \overline{K}$ and $L_m(S/G) = K$ if and only if:

1. $K$ is controllable with respect to $G$ and $\Sigma_u$;
2. $K$ is prefix-closed;
3. $K$ is nonblocking.

Proof. $(\Leftarrow)$ If $K$ is controllable, prefix-closed, and nonblocking, define

S(s) = \begin{cases} \Sigma & \text{if } s \in \overline{K} \\ \Sigma_u & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Then $L(S/G) = \overline{K}$ and $L_m(S/G) = K$.

$(\Rightarrow)$ If $S$ exists with $L(S/G) = \overline{K}$, then for every $s \in \overline{K}$ and $\sigma \in \Sigma_u$ with $s\sigma \in L(G)$, we have $s\sigma \in L(S/G) = \overline{K}$. Hence $K$ is controllable. Prefix-closure follows from the definition of $L(S/G)$. Nonblocking follows from $L_m(S/G) = K$. $\square$

Corollary G.1.1 (Authority Grants in $\Sigma_c$). All authority-granting actions must reside in $\Sigma_c$.

Proof. Suppose an authority-granting action were in $\Sigma_u$. Then the language $K$ containing no such grants would not be controllable, since an uncontrollable grant could occur outside the supervisor's control. Hence no supervisor could enforce the absence of unauthorized grants. $\square$

G.3 Fail-Safe Invariance

Theorem G.2 (Fail-Safe Invariance). If all hazardous transitions are triggered by controllable events ($\Sigma_{\mathrm{hazard}} \subseteq \Sigma_c$), then under loss of synchronization the fail-safe supervisor $S_{\mathrm{safe}} = \Sigma_u$ cannot transition into a hazardous state.

Proof. A transition to a hazardous state requires some $\sigma_h \in \Sigma_{\mathrm{hazard}} \subseteq \Sigma_c$. Upon asserting $\bot$, the control pattern is $S_{\mathrm{safe}} = \Sigma_u$. Since $\Sigma_c \cap \Sigma_u = \emptyset$, $\sigma_h \notin S_{\mathrm{safe}}$. Thus $\delta(q, \sigma_h)$ is disabled in $S_{\mathrm{safe}}/G$. $\square$

