Appendix H: Cryptographic Governance Integrity

H.1 Signature Scheme

Definition H.1 (Signature Scheme). A signature scheme is a triple $\Sigma = (\mathrm{Gen}, \mathrm{Sign}, \mathrm{Verify})$ where:

· $\mathrm{Gen}(1^\lambda) \to (pk, sk)$ generates a key pair;
· $\mathrm{Sign}_{sk}(m) \to \sigma$ signs a message;
· $\mathrm{Verify}_{pk}(m, \sigma) \to \{0, 1\}$ verifies a signature.

Definition H.2 (EUF-CMA Security). A signature scheme is existentially unforgeable under chosen-message attack if for every probabilistic polynomial-time adversary $\mathcal{A}$,

\Pr\left[\mathrm{Verify}_{pk}(m^*, \sigma^*) = 1 \ \middle| \ \begin{array}{l} (pk, sk) \leftarrow \mathrm{Gen}(1^\lambda) \\ (m^*, \sigma^*) \leftarrow \mathcal{A}^{\mathrm{Sign}_{sk}(\cdot)}(pk) \\ m^* \notin \mathcal{Q} \end{array}\right] \leq \mathrm{negl}(\lambda),

where $\mathcal{Q}$ is the set of messages queried to the signing oracle.

H.2 Governance Events

Definition H.3 (Governance Event Message). A governance event message is

m_t = (\mathrm{type}, \Delta A, \Delta P, \mathrm{issuer}, \mathrm{epoch}, \mathrm{timestamp}, H(P_t), H(P_{t+1})).

Definition H.4 (Governance Event). A governance event is

g_t = \langle m_t, \sigma_t \rangle, \qquad \sigma_t = \mathrm{Sign}_{sk_I}(m_t).

Definition H.5 (Replay Protection). A governance event $g_t$ is valid at time $t'$ if:

1. $\mathrm{Verify}_{pk_I}(m_t, \sigma_t) = 1$;
2. $\mathrm{epoch}_t > \mathrm{epoch}_{t'}$ for all previously accepted events $g_{t'}$;
3. $|\mathrm{timestamp}_t - t'| \leq \Delta_{\mathrm{fresh}}$.

H.3 Trust Assumptions

Assumption H.1 (Cryptographic Trust).

1. $\Sigma$ is EUF-CMA-secure;
2. $sk_I$ is stored in a hardware security module and is inaccessible to the agent;
3. Signature verification is correct;
4. The institutional authority is not compromised;
5. No legitimate signature can be obtained by manipulating the signer;
6. Replay and rollback protections are enforced.

H.4 Security Reduction

Theorem H.1 (Capability–Authority Non-Implication). Under Assumption H.1, the probability that any sequence of agent actions produces a valid governance event $g_t$ with $\sigma_t$ verifiable under $pk_I$ is bounded by $\mathrm{negl}(\lambda)$.

Proof. Suppose for contradiction that there exists a polynomial-time agent algorithm $\mathcal{A}$ that produces a valid governance event $g_t$ with non-negligible probability. Construct an adversary $\mathcal{B}$ for EUF-CMA as follows:

1. $\mathcal{B}$ receives $pk_I$ from the EUF-CMA challenger.
2. $\mathcal{B}$ simulates the GCP environment for $\mathcal{A}$, using the signing oracle to answer any legitimate signing requests (but only for messages not controlled by $\mathcal{A}$).
3. When $\mathcal{A}$ outputs $(m^*, \sigma^*)$ with $\mathrm{Verify}_{pk_I}(m^*, \sigma^*) = 1$ and $m^*$ not previously queried, $\mathcal{B}$ outputs $(m^*, \sigma^*)$ as its forgery.

By Assumption H.1 (2), the agent does not possess $sk_I$, so $\mathcal{A}$ cannot produce valid signatures except by forgery or by receiving them from a legitimate signer. Assumption H.1 (5) rules out signer manipulation. Assumption H.1 (6) rules out replay. Hence the probability that $\mathcal{A}$ succeeds is bounded by the EUF-CMA advantage of $\mathcal{B}$, which is $\mathrm{negl}(\lambda)$. $\square$

Corollary H.1.1 (Non-Self-Transition). Under Assumption H.1, no autonomous agent may transition the system from $\mathrm{REVOKED}$ to $\mathrm{ACTIVE}$ without an external authorized governance event.

Proof. By Theorem H.1, any such transition requires a signed governance event verifiable under $pk_I$. The agent cannot produce the required signature except with negligible probability. $\square$

