Appendix I: Coalition Non-Amplification

I.1 Graph Formalization

Definition I.1 (Communication Graph). The communication graph $G_{\mathrm{comm}} = (\mathcal{C}, E_{\mathrm{comm}})$ is an undirected graph on the agent set $\mathcal{C}$.

Definition I.2 (Delegation Graph). The delegation graph $G_{\mathrm{del}} = (\mathcal{C}, E_{\mathrm{del}})$ is a directed graph on the agent set $\mathcal{C}$, with edges signed by the delegating agent.

Definition I.3 (Coalition Capability). For a coalition $\mathcal{C}$ with communication graph $G_{\mathrm{comm}}$,

K_{\mathcal{C}} = f\left(\bigcup_{i \in \mathcal{C}} K_i, G_{\mathrm{comm}}\right),

where $f$ is monotone increasing in the number of communication edges $|E_{\mathrm{comm}}|$ and in the union of individual capabilities.

Definition I.4 (Coalition Authority).

A_{\mathcal{C}} = \begin{cases} \bigsqcup_{i \in \mathcal{C}} A_i & \text{if } G_{\mathrm{del}} \text{ contains a valid delegation path with signatures} \\ \bot & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

I.2 Non-Amplification

Theorem I.1 (Coalition Non-Amplification). There exist coalitions with arbitrarily large $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ and $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bot$, and coalitions with $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bigsqcup_i A_i$ and $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ equal to the sum of individual capabilities. Consequently $K_{\mathcal{C}} \nRightarrow A_{\mathcal{C}}$.

Proof. First family. Let $\mathcal{C} = \{1, \ldots, n\}$ with $G_{\mathrm{comm}}$ complete and $G_{\mathrm{del}}$ empty. Then $K_{\mathcal{C}} = f(\bigcup_i K_i, K_n)$ where $K_n$ is the complete graph on $n$ vertices. As $n \to \infty$, $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ grows without bound. But $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bot$ since $E_{\mathrm{del}} = \emptyset$.

Second family. Let $\mathcal{C} = \{1, \ldots, n\}$ with $G_{\mathrm{del}}$ a star centered at agent 1 with signed edges $1 \to i$ for all $i$, and $G_{\mathrm{comm}} = G_{\mathrm{del}}$. Then $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bigsqcup_i A_i$ since there exist valid delegation paths. But $K_{\mathcal{C}} = f(\bigcup_i K_i, G_{\mathrm{star}})$, which is bounded by a function of the sum of individual capabilities, since the communication graph is a star, not complete.

Hence $K_{\mathcal{C}} \nRightarrow A_{\mathcal{C}}$. $\square$

